s-none">Git itself doesn=E2=80=99t strictly require a Linux/Unix shell=E2=
=80=94it runs natively on Windows, macOS, and Linux without the need for vi=
rtualization. However, many Git commands are designed to be used in a Unix-=
like environment, so having access to a Bash shell can make certain workflo=
ws more seamless.</span></p><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none">On Windo=
ws, Git ships with <strong>Git Bash</strong>, which provides a Unix-like te=
rminal without requiring a full virtualized Linux system. You can also use =
Git within <strong>Windows Command Prompt (cmd)</strong> or <strong>PowerSh=
ell</strong>, but some commands (like complex scripting with pipes and alia=
ses) may work more intuitively in Git Bash or Windows Subsystem for Linux (=
<strong>WSL</strong>).</span></p><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none">If =
you wanted a more native Linux/Unix shell, <strong>WSL</strong> could be an=
 option, but that=E2=80=99s not virtualization=E2=80=94it=E2=80=99s a compa=
tibility layer that lets you run Linux binaries on Windows. If you were run=
ning a full Linux distro via a virtual machine (VM), then <strong>that</str=
ong> would require virtualization.</span></p><p><span class=3D"font-ligatur=
